<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fréjus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Forum Julii</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fréjus is a town in the Var <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast. It began as Forum Julii, a market town founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> or his heir, and under Augustus it became a Roman colony and naval base where ships captured at Actium were stationed. Its Roman amphitheatre, aqueduct and early Christian baptistery survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frejus.jpg" alt="Fréjus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cyrilb1881 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MairieFr%C3%A9jus.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fréjus lies in the Var department on the coast of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, next to Saint-Raphaël, with which it forms one urban agglomeration, and below the Massif de l'Esterel. It stood at an important crossroads formed by the Via Julia Augusta, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>, and the Via Domitia. Tacitus called its port claustra maris, the gateway to the sea. Its origins probably lie with the Celto-Ligurian people who settled round the natural harbour of Aegytna, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaeans">Phocaeans</a> of Marseille later set up an outpost on the site.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> refounded the city as Forum Julii to counter Massalia, the later Marseille, which had sided with Pompeius in the civil war. The date is unclear, but 49 BCE is most likely. Octavius brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> at Actium in 31 BCE to Forum Julii, and between 29 and 27 BCE it became a colony for veterans of the 8th legion. Augustus made it the capital of the new province of Narbonensis in 22 BCE, and its port became the only naval base of the Roman fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> until the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> the major monuments were built: the amphitheatre, the aqueduct, the lighthouse, the baths and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. The walls, 3.7 km long, protected 35 hectares and about six thousand inhabitants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Gnaeus Julius Agricola</a>, later the conqueror of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, was born there in 40 CE. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> was elected in 374, and Saint Léonce, bishop from 433, built the first cathedral with its baptistery. Muslim raiders attacked repeatedly between the 7th and 9th centuries, and silt clogged the port and left a swampy plain between the town and the sea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Fréjus has the richest concentration of Roman architecture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>. Its amphitheatre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> survive, and the aqueduct from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a>, 42 km long, is largely well preserved. The baptistery of the cathedral dates from the 5th century, and remains of the ancient harbour, with the lighthouse and the quay of Augustus, can be seen. The Malpasset Dam above the town ruptured on 2 December 1959, killing over 400 people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> landed at Fréjus on 9 October 1799, returning from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The town is a resort that hosts three to four times its regular population in summer.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fréjus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their land was given to Massalia and later absorbed into Gallia Narbonensis, where the Roman colony of <b>Forum Julii</b> (Fréjus) was founded nearby.</blockquote><a href="/state/oxybii">Oxybii</a></li></ul></section>
